-- -*- mode: lua -*-

-- MIDI map for the Novation Launchkey MK3 keyboards.
-- Albert Gräf <agggraef@gmail.com>, 20 April 2024

-- This contains the default MIDI map for the Launchkey driver, as well as
-- some rudimentary bindings for the buttons and knobs in standalone mode
-- (these are only active if the Launchkey driver is turned off). To use this,
-- load the map in Raptor and connect the Launchkey to Pd's first MIDI
-- input. In addition, to use the built-in driver, also connect the second
-- (DAW) port to Pd's second port, on both input and output. The map assumes
-- the factory setup for the knobs (CC21-28).

-- STANDALONE:

-- The standalone map binds all buttons that can be accessed without DAW mode
-- on a Mini MK3. Note that the bigger Launchkey MK3 models have a lot more
-- mappable buttons, you're free to map those in addition to the buttons
-- listed below. The standalone map, as well as the driver's DAW mode map
-- described below, were chosen so that they will work on *any* Launchkey MK3
-- device, and the Mini MK3 is the most limited of these.

-- Bindings with Raptor functions given in parentheses:
-- - The (unshifted) ">" and "Stop/Solo/Mute" buttons (latch, mute), these also
--   work in DAW mode.
-- - The transport buttons (play, loop).
-- - The (shifted) track left/right buttons (rewind, pulse).

-- The knobs are mapped as in the first fader bank of the Launchpad, i.e.,
-- from left to right: 1 = pitch hi, 2 = pitch lo, 3 = pattern mode (random,
-- up, down, etc.), 4 = pos/anacrusis (the "scrubbing" control), 5 = tempo,
-- 6 = meter numerator, 7 = meter denominator, 8 = division. These bindings
-- will also work in DAW mode if you choose the first Custom knob mode.

-- DAW MODE:

-- The DAW mode binding works pretty much like the Launchpad one (within the
-- limitations of the device), with the knobs and faders mapped to the same
-- five banks of Raptor controls under the corresponding knob modes (Volume,
-- Pan, Send A+B, Device), and the 2x8 grid functioning either as launch or
-- drum pads, depending on the pad mode (Session or Drum). Just like on the
-- Launchpad, the pads are fully mappable in Session pad mode, and the default
-- binding matches the two bottom rows of the Launchpad's grid.

-- Note that the faders are only available on the Launchkey 49 MK3 and up. By
-- default, the knobs are mapped to the Volume bank, while the faders are
-- mapped to the Device bank. You can change this with the shifted mode pads
-- for the knobs, and with the shifted track select buttons for the faders.
-- There are some restrictions concerning the faders: They cannot be set to
-- the same mode as the knobs, and Pan mode isn't available for the faders.
-- Finally, the ninth fader is special: It maps to CC7 no matter which fader
-- mode you're in, and can thus be used to control the volume of a synth
-- connected to the first output port.

-- The arrow keys let you select Raptor instances (left, right) and presets
-- (up, down). Note that on the Mini MK3 you'll have to press Shift to get
-- these. On the larger models (Launchkey 25 MK3 and up), holding the Device
-- Select button brings up a strip of up to eight blue pads in the top row
-- which you can use to select a Raptor instance at random; it also highlights
-- the currently selected instance in pink, just like the strip of track
-- selection buttons on the Launchpad Pro. On the Launchkey 49 MK3 and up, you
-- can also use the track select buttons for this purpose. (These are all
-- hard-wired bindings provided by the driver.)

-- The transport buttons work as well (play, loop); on the Mini these also
-- light up to reflect the current transport/loop playback state (on the
-- larger models these buttons have no backlight). The Stop button will work
-- in DAW mode as well (it stops the transport if it is currently rolling).
-- This button is only available on the bigger Launchkey devices, but on the
-- Mini you can press Shift+Play instead to get this function.

-- Moreover, Raptor's built-in metronome click can be turned on and off with
-- the Click button available on the larger Launchkey models. (There's no
-- ready-made binding for this on the Launchkey Mini, but it should be a piece
-- of cake to just map it to one of the session pads instead.)

-- Last but not least, the larger models (Launchkey 25 MK3 and up) also have a
-- little LCD screen which the driver uses to show useful information: a
-- sign-on message during startup; knob and fader values (including pickup)
-- while twiddling the knobs and faders; pad functions while pushing the pads;
-- and presets and instances whenever the selection changes. Also, the Device
-- Lock button can be used to recall information about the currently selected
-- instance and preset, or "omni" if no instance is currently selected. (This
-- button doesn't lock anything in Raptor, it just displays information.)

-- DRUM GRID:

-- DAW mode also enables Raptor's built-in drum grid. You get this by pressing
-- Shift along with the "Drum" pad. Raptor uses the Launchkey's default drum
-- grid layout featuring two side-by-side 2x4 grids which together form a 4x4
-- matrix, with note 36 on channel 10 in the lower left corner:

-- 40 41 42 43 | 48 49 50 51
-- 36 37 38 39 | 44 45 46 47

-- As you can see, if you stitch together the left and right 2x4 groups, you
-- get the usual 4x4 grid found on many drum pad devices. Raptor shows the
-- right-hand 2x4 group in a darker color so that the layout becomes apparent.

-- In Drum mode, you can scroll through the six available 4x4 grids with the
-- up and down keys. These will give you the same four grids as on the
-- Launchpad, using the same color scheme, and two extra grids below that.
-- All in all, this amounts to a total of 96 different notes to play, ranging
-- from note 4 (E-1) to 99 (D#7), which should be more than enough to cover an
-- entire GM drum kit or whatever your synth or groovebox may provide.

-- Note that while Drum mode is active, the up and down arrow keys are
-- repurposed to switch between the six drum grids. To make them work for
-- Raptor preset selection again, you need to switch away from Drum mode
-- (e.g., to Session mode).

{
  -- standalone knob bindings, these work the same in session mode if you
  -- choose the Custom knob mode
  [21]  = { [1] = "pitchhi" },
  [22]  = { [1] = "pitchlo" },
  [23]  = { [1] = "mode" },
  [24]  = { [1] = "pos" },
  [25]  = { [1] = "tempo" },
  [26]  = { [1] = "meter-num" },
  [27]  = { [1] = "meter-denom" },
  [28]  = { [1] = "division" },
  -- standalone button assignments (pulse/rewind only work in standalone mode,
  -- the others also in session mode which also has alternative launch grid
  -- bindings for all these functions)
  [102] = { [16] = { "pulse", true } },
  [103] = { [16] = { "rewind", true } },
  [104] = { [16] = { "latch", true } },
  [105] = { [16] = { "mute", true } },
  [115] = { [16] = { "play", true } },
  [117] = { [16] = { "loop", true } },
  -- session mode bindings (only active if the launchkey driver is turned on)
  -- send A (min value) faders
  [13] = { [32] = "minvel" },
  [14] = { [32] = "pmin" },
  [15] = { [32] = "wmin" },
  [16] = { [32] = "gate" },
  [17] = { [32] = "hmin" },
  [18] = { [32] = "pref" },
  [19] = { [32] = "smin" },
  [20] = { [32] = "uniq" },
  -- send B (max value) faders
  [29] = { [32] = "maxvel" },
  [30] = { [32] = "pmax" },
  [31] = { [32] = "wmax" },
  [32] = { [32] = "gate" },
  [33] = { [32] = "hmax" },
  [34] = { [32] = "pref" },
  [35] = { [32] = "smax" },
  [36] = { [32] = "nmax" },
  -- pan (bipolar mod) faders
  [49] = { [32] = "velmod" },
  [50] = { [32] = "pmod" },
  [51] = { [32] = "gain" },
  [52] = { [32] = "gatemod" },
  [53] = { [32] = "hmod" },
  [54] = { [32] = "prefmod" },
  [55] = { [32] = "smod" },
  [56] = { [32] = "nmod" },
  -- volume faders
  [77] = { [32] = "pitchhi" },
  [78] = { [32] = "pitchlo" },
  [79] = { [32] = "mode" },
  [80] = { [32] = "pos" },
  [81] = { [32] = "tempo" },
  [82] = { [32] = "meter-num" },
  [83] = { [32] = "meter-denom" },
  [84] = { [32] = "division" },
  -- device mode faders (selection of most frequently used controls)
  [21]  = { [32] = "mode" },
  [22]  = { [32] = "pos" },
  [23]  = { [32] = "gain" },
  [24]  = { [32] = "gate" },
  [25]  = { [32] = "hmin" },
  [26]  = { [32] = "hmax" },
  [27]  = { [32] = "pref" },
  [28]  = { [32] = "prefmod" },
  -- metronome click (Click button)
  [68]  = { [32] = { "click", true } },
  -- alternative bindings for latch/mute, same as in standalone
  -- NOTE: standalone bindings repeated here, so that they aren't overridden
  [104] = { [17] = { "latch", true }, [16] = { "latch", true } },
  [105] = { [17] = { "mute", true }, [16] = { "mute", true } },
  -- transport
  -- NOTE: standalone bindings repeated here, so that they aren't overridden
  [115] = { [32] = { "play", true }, [16] = { "play", true } },
  [117] = { [32] = { "loop", true }, [16] = { "loop", true } },
  -- predefined pads (session view)
  -- 240, 241 unassigned
  [242] = { [17] = { "raptor", true } },
  [243] = { [17] = { "rewind", true } },
  -- 244 unassigned
  [245] = { [17] = { "loop-prev", true } },
  [246] = { [17] = { "loop-next", true } },
  [247] = { [17] = { "play", true } },
  -- upper row
  [224] = { [17] = { "mute", true } },
  [225] = { [17] = { "latch", true } },
  [226] = { [17] = { "bypass", true } },
  -- 227, 228 unassigned
  [229] = { [17] = { "loop-load", true } },
  [230] = { [17] = { "loop-save", true } },
  [231] = { [17] = { "loop", true } },
}
